<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!DOCTYPE mapper
  PUBLIC "-//mybatis.org//DTD Mapper 3.0//EN"
  "https://mybatis.org/dtd/mybatis-3-mapper.dtd">
<mapper namespace="com.memox.tag.persistence.TagMapper">

  <resultMap id="tag" type="com.memox.tag.entity.Tag">
    <constructor>
      <idArg column="id" javaType="java.lang.String"/>
      <arg column="name" javaType="java.lang.String"/>
      <arg column="name_folded" javaType="java.lang.String"/>
      <arg column="created_at" javaType="java.time.Instant"/>
    </constructor>
  </resultMap>

  <resultMap id="tagCatalogEntry" type="com.memox.tag.entity.TagCatalogEntry">
    <constructor>
      <idArg column="id" javaType="java.lang.String"/>
      <arg column="name" javaType="java.lang.String"/>
      <!-- _long, not long: MyBatis' registry maps the bare name to java.lang.Long, and a wrapper
           against a record's primitive component is the mismatch that shipped an HTTP 500 once. -->
      <arg column="card_count" javaType="_long"/>
    </constructor>
  </resultMap>

  <!--
    The management catalog (BR-230): every tag, with how many ACTIVE cards carry it.

    **The count joins `cards`, and Drift's does not.** `tagCatalog` counts `card_tags` rows alone,
    so a card sitting in Trash keeps inflating its tags' counts — and BR-237 says a hidden card
    must not count towards the catalog. The join is the fix; the WBS carries the same fix against
    the Flutter query, and Task 15 records the divergence.

    **Still a correlated scalar subquery, not a LEFT JOIN … GROUP BY.** A tag no card carries has
    to keep its row with a count of zero (BR-230) — it is the row the delete action exists for —
    and the subquery gives that without an outer join. `idx_card_tags_tag` leads on `tag_id`, so
    each count is an index lookup.

    **`name_folded ASC, id ASC` and the folded column is load-bearing.** Ordering by the spelling
    puts `Verb` before `adjective` in a byte-ordered collation and reads as random to whoever typed
    both. The folded value is the one BR-93's uniqueness is measured in, so the catalog's order and
    its identity agree by construction.

    Translation row 4: `instr(x, y) > 0` becomes `strpos(x, y) > 0`. `LIKE` would read a `%` in a
    tag name as a wildcard. The empty term is the resting state and matches everything, which is why
    the guard is `= ''` rather than a second statement.

    No `owner_id` predicate: this API has no principal to fill one (AD-03), and threading a
    permanently-null parameter through every layer would be a fourth spelling of "not yet".
  -->
  <select id="findTagCatalog" resultMap="tagCatalogEntry">
    SELECT t.id AS id
         , t.name AS name
         , (SELECT COUNT(*)
              FROM card_tags ct
              INNER JOIN cards c ON c.id = ct.card_id
             WHERE ct.tag_id = t.id AND c.delete_batch_id IS NULL) AS card_count
      FROM tags t
     WHERE (#{searchFolded} = '' OR strpos(t.name_folded, #{searchFolded}) &gt; 0)
     ORDER BY t.name_folded ASC
            , t.id ASC
  </select>

  <!--
    Does this name already exist (BR-93)?

    Matched on `name_folded`, never on `name` — the folded column is the one the unique index is
    built on and the only comparison that treats `Động từ` and `động từ` as one tag.
  -->
  <select id="findTagByFoldedName" resultMap="tag">
    SELECT id
         , name
         , name_folded
         , created_at FROM tags WHERE name_folded = #{nameFolded}
  </select>

  <!-- Read inside the rename/delete transaction, so the row acted on is the row that exists now. -->
  <select id="findTagById" resultMap="tag">
    SELECT id
         , name
         , name_folded
         , created_at FROM tags WHERE id = #{tagId}
  </select>

  <insert id="insertTag" parameterType="com.memox.tag.entity.Tag">
    INSERT INTO tags (id, name, name_folded, created_at)
    VALUES (#{id}, #{name}, #{nameFolded}, #{createdAt})
  </insert>

  <!--
    BR-233's rename: the two name columns, on the tag's own row.

    The id is never rewritten and `card_tags` is never touched, which is the whole point of the
    no-collision path — every card keeps the tag it had. A rename done as delete-and-recreate would
    drop the links and silently untag every card.
  -->
  <update id="renameTagById">
    UPDATE tags SET name = #{name}
  , name_folded = #{nameFolded} WHERE id = #{tagId}
  </update>

  <!--
    BR-234's merge, first half: give the target every card the source has and the target does not.

    Translation row 3: SQLite's `INSERT OR IGNORE` becomes `ON CONFLICT … DO NOTHING`. The pair is
    the primary key, so a card carrying both tags is skipped by the table's own identity rather
    than by a read-then-decide that would need a round trip per card and could still race.

    A merge therefore never raises any card's tag count (BR-94): a card that had only the source
    trades one for one, and a card that had both ends up with one fewer once the source links go.

    **No join to `cards`.** BR-237's other half: a card hidden in Trash keeps its links through a
    merge, so restoring it gets the tag back instead of a card quietly stripped of its metadata.
  -->
  <insert id="linkCardsOfTagTo">
    INSERT INTO card_tags (card_id, tag_id)
    SELECT ct.card_id
         , #{targetTagId} FROM card_tags ct WHERE ct.tag_id = #{sourceTagId}
    ON CONFLICT (card_id, tag_id) DO NOTHING
  </insert>

  <!--
    BR-235's delete, and the second half of BR-234's merge.

    Explicit, not left to the `ON DELETE CASCADE` on `card_tags.tag_id`: the cascade does the same
    thing, and that is the problem — a rule that says "unlink every card" should be a statement that
    unlinks every card, not a constraint someone could relax without reading this.
  -->
  <delete id="unlinkAllCardsFromTag">
    DELETE FROM card_tags WHERE tag_id = #{tagId}
  </delete>

  <delete id="deleteTagById">
    DELETE FROM tags WHERE id = #{tagId}
  </delete>

  <!-- The active cards of a batch, counted so a tombstoned or absent id refuses the batch. -->
  <select id="countActiveCardsByIds" resultType="long">
    SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cards
     WHERE id IN
    <foreach collection="cardIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="cardId">#{cardId}</foreach>
       AND delete_batch_id IS NULL
  </select>

  <!--
    Which cards of the batch already carry this tag, so linking is idempotent (BR-166) and the
    ceiling counts only the cards that would actually gain one.
  -->
  <select id="findCardsAlreadyTagged" resultType="string">
    SELECT ct.card_id FROM card_tags ct
     WHERE ct.tag_id = #{tagId}
       AND ct.card_id IN
    <foreach collection="cardIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="cardId">#{cardId}</foreach>
  </select>

  <!--
    Which cards are already at BR-94's ceiling.

    Drift asks this as `tagCountsForCards` — a count per card, folded into the cap check in Dart.
    The rule only ever asks "which of these is full", so the HAVING answers it in the statement and
    the service compares two id sets instead of a map of numbers it does not otherwise use.
  -->
  <select id="findCardsAtTagCeiling" resultType="string">
    SELECT ct.card_id FROM card_tags ct
     WHERE ct.card_id IN
    <foreach collection="cardIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="cardId">#{cardId}</foreach>
     GROUP BY ct.card_id
    HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= #{ceiling}
  </select>

  <!--
    One tag onto a batch, idempotent by the primary key.

    Drift links one row at a time because it has already filtered the batch down to the cards that
    would gain something; the same filter is what `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` does here, in one
    statement rather than one per card.
  -->
  <insert id="linkTagToCards">
    INSERT INTO card_tags (card_id, tag_id)
    SELECT c.id
         , #{tagId} FROM cards c
     WHERE c.id IN
    <foreach collection="cardIds" open="(" separator="," close=")" item="cardId">#{cardId}</foreach>
    ON CONFLICT (card_id, tag_id) DO NOTHING
  </insert>

  <!--
    The chips on one card, with their ids.

    Every card projection carries tag NAMES, which is what a chip draws; detaching one needs the id.
    Resolving name back to id in the client would mean folding there — the second normaliser BR-230
    forbids — so the id comes from the server that owns the fold.

    Ordered by the spelling, not the folded name: this is a display list, and BR-230's folded
    ordering is the catalog's rule because the catalog is also where identity is compared.
  -->
  <select id="findTagsForCard" resultMap="tag">
    SELECT t.id
         , t.name
         , t.name_folded
         , t.created_at
      FROM card_tags ct
      INNER JOIN tags t ON t.id = ct.tag_id
     WHERE ct.card_id = #{cardId}
     ORDER BY t.name ASC
            , t.id ASC
  </select>

  <delete id="unlinkTag">
    DELETE FROM card_tags WHERE card_id = #{cardId} AND tag_id = #{tagId}
  </delete>

</mapper>
